{"causes":["The early 20th-century analytic philosophy movement laid the groundwork for ordinary language philosophy's emergence.","Ludwig Wittgenstein's later works, particularly 'Philosophical Investigations', provided a critical framework that influenced ordinary language thinkers.","The reaction against formalism and abstraction in philosophy during the post-World War II era created a fertile environment for ordinary language approaches."],"effects":["Ordinary language philosophy significantly influenced the development of modern linguistics and cognitive science.","The methods established by this philosophy have been applied in various fields, including legal interpretation and therapy.","This philosophical approach led to a reevaluation of many traditional philosophical problems, shifting focus to language use in everyday contexts."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"analytic-philosophy","sourceName":"Analytic Philosophy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"ordinary-language-philosophy","targetName":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","context":"Ordinary language philosophy evolved from the principles established by analytic philosophy in the early 20th century."},{"sourceSlug":"philosophical-investigations","sourceName":"Philosophical Investigations","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"ordinary-language-philosophy","targetName":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","context":"Wittgenstein's 'Philosophical Investigations' is a seminal text that articulates the core tenets of ordinary language philosophy."},{"sourceSlug":"speech-act-theory","sourceName":"Speech Act Theory","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"ordinary-language-philosophy","targetName":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","context":"J.L. Austin's development of speech act theory in the 1950s created a foundational aspect of ordinary language philosophy."},{"sourceSlug":"united-states","sourceName":"United States","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"ordinary-language-philosophy","targetName":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","context":"The rise of ordinary language philosophy occurred significantly in the United States during the mid-20th century."},{"sourceSlug":"linguistics","sourceName":"Linguistics","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"ordinary-language-philosophy","targetName":"Ordinary Language Philosophy","context":"Ordinary language philosophy collaborates with linguistics by emphasizing the importance of language use in understanding meaning."}],"places":[{"name":"Cambridge, United Kingdom","role":"Major intellectual hub"},{"name":"Oxford, United Kingdom","role":"Development center"},{"name":"New York City, United States","role":"Cultural center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: ordinary language philosophy","type":"Reference article","slug":"ordinary-language-philosophy"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1056732","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordinary_language_philosophy"],"tags":["methodology","Political & Polemical Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Ordinary language philosophy (also 'linguistic philosophy' or the 'Oxford Philosophy' movement) is a philosophical approach that dominated British philosophy, particularly at the University of Oxford, from approximately the late 1930s to the early 1960s — associated primarily with Gilbert Ryle (1900–1976), J. L. Austin (1911–1960), P. F. Strawson (1919–2006), and the later work of Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951, particularly the Philosophical Investigations, 1953). The movement's central claim was that many traditional philosophical problems — questions about the existence of the external world, the nature of mental states, the problem of other minds, the analysis of knowledge and perception — arose from the systematic misuse of language: philosophers had taken words (like 'mind', 'know', 'perceive', 'cause') out of their ordinary contexts of use and forced them into frameworks that generated apparent paradoxes, when in fact the correct response was to attend carefully to how these words actually function in ordinary language.

The methodological core of ordinary language philosophy was close attention to the nuances of everyday linguistic use — Austin's 'linguistic phenomenology' (his preferred term) involved detailed analysis of the distinctions made by ordinary English speakers in contexts of action, perception, knowledge, and speech — producing the theory of speech acts (How to Do Things with Words, 1962, published posthumously from Austin's William James Lectures at Harvard, 1955). Austin distinguished between locutionary acts (the act of saying something), illocutionary acts (what one does in saying something: promising, warning, asserting, threatening), and perlocutionary acts (the effects of saying something), providing the foundational framework for what became philosophy of language and pragmatics.

Ordinary language philosophy was the dominant mode of British academic philosophy in the 1940s–1960s and had a significant influence on philosophy of mind (Ryle's critique of Cartesian 'category mistakes' in The Concept of Mind, 1949), epistemology (Strawson's Individuals, 1959), and philosophy of language (Austin's speech act theory). Its decline from the mid-1960s — associated with the rise of formal semantics (Chomsky, Davidson) and the analytic philosophy's turn toward more formal methods — was followed by the absorption of speech act theory into linguistics and pragmatics.","subjects":["Philosophy","Modern Era","Ludwig Wittgenstein","J.L. Austin","Philosophy of Language","20th Century","Oxford Philosophy","Analytic Philosophy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Ordinary language philosophy redefined philosophical inquiry into language, affecting fields like linguistics and cognitive science, and influencing contemporary thought on meaning and communication.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
